-- 099: Hand-written IELTS seed content (ROADMAP_M3 §3.2)
-- IELTS reading (8) and listening (10) were too thin for the bulk generator
-- to few-shot from. These 10 hand-authored sets raise the seed pool so
-- constrained AI generation can fill the rest. Shapes mirror existing rows:
--   grouped_reading: passage_text + questions[{question_text, options, answers}]
--   grouped_listening: passage_script (multi-speaker, TTS on demand) + questions

-- ─── READING 1: Urban beekeeping ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','reading','grouped_reading','Urban Beekeeping',
'{"type":"grouped_reading","passage_text":"Over the past two decades, beekeeping has moved from the countryside to the city. Rooftops in London, Paris and Tokyo now host thousands of hives, and city councils that once banned the practice actively encourage it. The shift is often explained as a response to the decline of wild pollinators, but the reality is more complicated. Urban bees frequently enjoy better health than their rural cousins. Cities offer a long flowering season, since parks, balconies and roadside plantings bloom from early spring to late autumn, and urban areas are largely free of the agricultural pesticides that have been linked to colony collapse. A study of hives in Birmingham found that urban colonies produced more honey per hive than colonies in the surrounding farmland, where vast fields of a single crop flower briefly and then offer nothing for months. Yet enthusiasm has created its own problem: density. In some districts of Paris there are now more hives per square kilometre than the available nectar can support, and beekeepers report falling yields as colonies compete. Some ecologists also warn that honeybees, which are essentially managed livestock, may crowd out the wild solitary bees that are often more efficient pollinators. The lesson, researchers suggest, is not that urban beekeeping is harmful, but that it is not automatically beneficial either. Like any form of land management, it requires planning: a city that wants bees must also plant the flowers to feed them.","questions":[{"question_text":"According to the passage, why do urban bees often do better than rural bees?","options":["Cities have fewer diseases affecting bees","Flowering plants are available for a longer period and pesticides are rarer","Urban beekeepers are more experienced","City temperatures are higher all year"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What problem has the popularity of city beekeeping created in Paris?","options":["Councils have started banning hives again","Honey quality has declined sharply","There are too many hives for the nectar available","Wild bees have completely disappeared"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"Urban colonies in Birmingham produced more honey than nearby rural colonies. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"Most city councils still prohibit beekeeping in residential areas. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Solitary wild bees sting people less often than honeybees. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["C"]}],"explanation":"Q1: the passage cites the long flowering season and absence of agricultural pesticides. Q2: density — more hives than nectar. Q3: stated directly. Q4: contradicted — councils now encourage it. Q5: stinging is never mentioned."}',
3,'reading_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── READING 2: The history of timekeeping ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','reading','grouped_reading','A Brief History of Timekeeping',
'{"type":"grouped_reading","passage_text":"For most of human history, time was something observed rather than measured. Communities rose with the sun and organised their days around its movement, and the earliest instruments, such as the Egyptian shadow clock of around 1500 BC, did little more than divide daylight into segments. The real transformation came with the mechanical clock in medieval Europe. Built first for monasteries, where prayer had to happen at fixed hours, these machines escaped to town squares within a century. The clock tower changed urban life profoundly: markets, courts and workshops began to run on shared, abstract hours rather than on daylight. Historians have argued that this shift did as much to prepare Europe for industrialisation as any invention of the eighteenth century, because factories required workers to sell their time in standard units. Precision, however, remained poor. Even the best pendulum clocks of the 1700s drifted by seconds each day, which mattered enormously at sea, where finding longitude depended on knowing the exact time at a reference point. The British government famously offered a fortune for a solution, and the carpenter John Harrison spent four decades perfecting a marine chronometer that lost only a few seconds on a voyage to Jamaica. Today the definition of a second rests not on the heavens or on machinery but on the behaviour of caesium atoms, which vibrate over nine billion times per second. Atomic clocks are accurate to within one second over millions of years, a precision so extreme that it has reversed the old relationship: the rotation of the Earth itself is now corrected against the clock.","questions":[{"question_text":"What was the original purpose of mechanical clocks in medieval Europe?","options":["Coordinating market trading hours","Marking fixed times for prayer in monasteries","Helping ships navigate at sea","Regulating factory working hours"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Why was accurate timekeeping important for sailors?","options":["It allowed them to calculate longitude","It helped them predict storms","It synchronised naval attacks","It regulated meals on board"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"John Harrison developed his chronometer in less than ten years. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Some historians link the mechanical clock to the rise of industrial society. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"Atomic clocks are too expensive for most countries to maintain. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["C"]}],"explanation":"Q1: built for monasteries where prayer happened at fixed hours. Q2: longitude depended on knowing exact reference time. Q3: contradicted — four decades. Q4: stated. Q5: cost never discussed."}',
3,'reading_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── READING 3: Language extinction ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','reading','grouped_reading','Vanishing Voices: Language Extinction',
'{"type":"grouped_reading","passage_text":"Linguists estimate that of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, nearly half will fall silent by the end of the century. A language dies, on average, every few months, usually not in a single dramatic moment but through a quiet generational slide: grandparents speak it fluently, parents understand but reply in a dominant language, and children grow up with only a handful of words. The causes are rarely mysterious. Economic opportunity, urban migration and national education systems all reward the use of major languages, and parents often make the entirely rational choice to raise children in the language of employment. What is lost, however, extends beyond vocabulary. Languages encode generations of local knowledge: Pacific navigation techniques, Amazonian plant medicine, and ways of classifying the natural world that have no equivalent elsewhere. When the last fluent speaker of a language dies, an unwritten library closes. Revival is difficult but not impossible. Hebrew, effectively unspoken in daily life for centuries, was rebuilt into the mother tongue of millions within three generations. More recently, Welsh has climbed back from steep decline through compulsory schooling and dedicated television channels, and the Maori language nests of New Zealand, where elders immerse preschool children in the language, have been copied around the world. Researchers emphasise one consistent finding: technology and documentation help, but languages survive only when families speak them at home. A dictionary can preserve a language the way a museum preserves a bird, but neither can make it fly again.","questions":[{"question_text":"How does language death typically happen, according to the passage?","options":["Governments ban minority languages","Speakers die in natural disasters","Each generation uses the language less than the one before","Writing systems are lost"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"The passage compares a documented but unspoken language to","options":["a closed library","a preserved bird in a museum","an empty nest","a forgotten dictionary"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Hebrew was revived as an everyday spoken language. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"The Maori language nest model has been adopted outside New Zealand. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"Most parents regret raising their children in a dominant language. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["C"]}],"explanation":"Q1: generational slide described in paragraph one. Q2: final sentence museum/bird analogy. Q3: stated — mother tongue of millions. Q4: copied around the world. Q5: regret never discussed."}',
4,'reading_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── READING 4: Desalination ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','reading','grouped_reading','Fresh Water from the Sea',
'{"type":"grouped_reading","passage_text":"As rivers shrink and aquifers empty, a growing number of coastal cities have turned to the one water source that never runs dry: the ocean. Desalination, the removal of salt from seawater, now supplies most of the drinking water in Israel and nearly half in Singapore, and more than twenty thousand plants operate worldwide. The dominant technology, reverse osmosis, forces seawater through membranes with pores so fine that salt molecules cannot pass. It replaced older thermal methods, which boiled seawater and condensed the vapour, because membranes use far less energy. Even so, energy remains the central obstacle. Pushing water through membranes at high pressure consumes three to four kilowatt hours per cubic metre, several times the cost of treating river water, and ties the price of drinking water to the price of electricity. The second problem leaves the plant by pipe: for every litre of fresh water produced, roughly a litre and a half of concentrated brine is returned to the sea. Discharged carelessly, this dense, salty plume sinks to the seabed and suffocates marine life around the outfall. Modern plants dilute brine with cooling water or spread it through diffusers, and some researchers see opportunity rather than waste, proposing to harvest the brine for lithium, magnesium and other valuable minerals. Engineers are confident that costs will continue to fall as membranes improve and as plants pair with solar farms, whose cheapest hours of generation can be matched to water production. Desalination will never be the whole answer to water scarcity, its advocates concede, but for a thirsty coastal city it has become the most reliable insurance policy available.","questions":[{"question_text":"Why did reverse osmosis replace thermal desalination methods?","options":["It produces cleaner water","It requires much less energy","It works in colder climates","It needs no maintenance"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What environmental risk does the passage associate with brine?","options":["It raises sea temperatures","It poisons drinking water supplies","It sinks and harms life on the seabed","It corrodes ships and pipelines"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"Israel obtains the majority of its drinking water from desalination. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"Some researchers propose extracting minerals from brine. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"Desalinated water is cheaper than treated river water. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["B"]}],"explanation":"Q1: membranes use far less energy. Q2: brine plume sinks and suffocates marine life. Q3: stated — most of the drinking water in Israel. Q4: lithium and magnesium harvesting proposed. Q5: contradicted — several times the cost of treating river water."}',
3,'reading_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── READING 5: The psychology of queuing ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','reading','grouped_reading','The Psychology of Waiting in Line',
'{"type":"grouped_reading","passage_text":"In the 1950s, managers of a New York office tower faced a stream of complaints about slow lifts. Engineers advised that nothing could be done: the machinery was already at its limit. The building solved the problem for almost nothing by installing mirrors beside the lift doors. Tenants, now occupied with their own reflections, stopped complaining, although the lifts were no faster than before. The episode became a founding story of queue psychology, a field built on a simple insight: the experience of waiting matters at least as much as its length. Researchers have since catalogued reliable patterns. Occupied time feels shorter than empty time, which is why theme parks wind their queues past screens and performers. Uncertain waits feel longer than known ones, which is why a sign announcing thirty minutes from this point calms people even when it is pessimistic. Unexplained waits breed the most anger of all: passengers sitting on a stationary train relax noticeably the moment a driver announces the reason for the delay. Fairness matters too. Studies in banks and airports show that a single serpentine queue feeding many counters is perceived as fairer than separate lines, because nobody who arrived later is served earlier, even though average waiting time is mathematically similar. Some economists argue that queues are best understood as a price paid in time rather than money, and note that people quietly calculate whether a wait is worth it just as they would a bill. Businesses have learned the lesson well. The modern obsession with progress bars, order tracking and constant status updates is, at heart, the mirror beside the lift: the wait is not shorter, but it no longer feels empty.","questions":[{"question_text":"How did the office building reduce complaints about its lifts?","options":["It upgraded the lift machinery","It installed mirrors so people had something to do while waiting","It limited the number of tenants per floor","It published the waiting times"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Why do single serpentine queues feel fairer than multiple lines?","options":["They are always faster on average","Customers can choose their own counter","Nobody who arrives later gets served earlier","They take up less space"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"Passengers become calmer when the cause of a delay is explained. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"Serpentine queues reduce average waiting time substantially. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Theme parks pay performers more than other employers do. (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN)","options":["TRUE","FALSE","NOT GIVEN"],"answers":["C"]}],"explanation":"Q1: mirrors occupied the tenants. Q2: nobody later is served earlier. Q3: stated — train passengers relax after announcement. Q4: contradicted — mathematically similar. Q5: pay never mentioned."}',
4,'reading_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── LISTENING 1: Gym membership enquiry (Section 1 style) ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','listening','grouped_listening','Gym Membership Enquiry',
'{"type":"grouped_listening","group_name":"IELTS Listening Section 1 - Gym Membership","direction":"Listen to the conversation and answer the questions.","passage_script":"Woman: Good afternoon, Riverside Fitness Centre. Man: Hello, I would like to ask about your membership options. Woman: Of course. We have three plans. The basic plan is twenty-five pounds a month and covers the gym floor only. The standard plan is thirty-eight pounds and adds all group classes. The premium plan is fifty pounds and includes the pool and sauna as well. Man: And is there a joining fee? Woman: There is a one-time fee of fifteen pounds, but it is waived if you sign up before the end of June. Man: I see. What are your opening hours? Woman: Weekdays six in the morning until ten at night, and weekends eight until six. Man: Could I try the gym before deciding? Woman: Certainly. We offer one free trial session. You just need to bring photo identification and book a slot in advance. Man: Perfect. Could I book a trial for Saturday morning? Woman: Saturday at nine is available. May I take your name? Man: It is Daniel Reed. R-E-E-D.","questions":[{"question_text":"How much does the standard plan cost per month?","options":["25 pounds","38 pounds","50 pounds","15 pounds"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What is included in the premium plan but not the standard plan?","options":["Group classes","The gym floor","The pool and sauna","Personal training"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"When is the joining fee waived?","options":["For students only","If you sign up before the end of June","On weekends","After the trial session"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What must the man bring to his trial session?","options":["Photo identification","A bank statement","Sports clothing","A referral letter"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"What time is the trial booked for?","options":["Saturday at eight","Saturday at nine","Sunday at nine","Saturday at six"],"answers":["B"]}],"explanation":"Standard = 38 pounds with classes; premium adds pool and sauna; fee waived before end of June; photo ID required; trial Saturday at nine."}',
2,'listening_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── LISTENING 2: Library orientation (Section 2 style) ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','listening','grouped_listening','University Library Orientation',
'{"type":"grouped_listening","group_name":"IELTS Listening Section 2 - Library Orientation","direction":"Listen to the talk and answer the questions.","passage_script":"Woman: Welcome to the Central Library orientation. Let me walk you through the building. The ground floor holds the help desk, the printing room, and the cafe, which by the way closes at four, earlier than the library itself. The first floor is our main collection. You can borrow up to eight books at a time as an undergraduate, and loans last three weeks. Renew online up to two times, but if a book is requested by another student, you must return it within five days. The second floor is the silent study area. No phones, no group work, and honestly we do enforce it. If you need to work in a team, book one of the twelve discussion rooms on the third floor through the library app, up to a week in advance. One more thing many students miss: the basement houses our media lab with video editing computers and recording booths, free for any registered student. Lost your student card? Replacements are handled not here but at the student services office in the Atrium building, and they charge ten pounds.","questions":[{"question_text":"How many books can an undergraduate borrow at one time?","options":["Five","Eight","Twelve","Three"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What happens if another student requests a book you have borrowed?","options":["You keep it until the loan ends","You must return it within five days","You pay a small fee","Your loan is renewed automatically"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Where are the discussion rooms located?","options":["The basement","The ground floor","The second floor","The third floor"],"answers":["D"]},{"question_text":"What is available in the basement?","options":["The cafe","A media lab with recording booths","The silent study area","The help desk"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Where can students replace a lost student card?","options":["The library help desk","The student services office","The printing room","The discussion rooms"],"answers":["B"]}],"explanation":"Eight books, three weeks; requested books back in five days; discussion rooms third floor via app; media lab in basement; card replacement at student services, ten pounds."}',
2,'listening_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── LISTENING 3: Lecture on sleep science (Section 4 style) ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','listening','grouped_listening','Lecture: The Science of Sleep',
'{"type":"grouped_listening","group_name":"IELTS Listening Section 4 - Sleep Science","direction":"Listen to the lecture and answer the questions.","passage_script":"Professor: Today I want to challenge the idea that sleep is simply rest. While you sleep, your brain is remarkably busy. During deep sleep, the brain clears waste products through a network discovered only recently, called the glymphatic system. Think of it as a nightly cleaning crew, flushing out proteins that, when they accumulate, are associated with memory disorders. Sleep also performs a sorting function. Experiences from the day are replayed and the important ones are transferred from temporary storage in the hippocampus to long-term storage in the cortex. This is why students who sleep after studying consistently outperform those who stay up revising: the material is literally being filed overnight. Now, how much sleep do we need? For adults, the evidence points firmly to between seven and nine hours. People who claim to thrive on five hours almost always underperform on tests of attention, although, interestingly, they are usually unaware of the decline. The most practical advice from the research is about regularity. Going to bed and waking at consistent times matters more than the occasional short night, because the body anticipates sleep and begins preparing hormones in advance. An irregular schedule keeps that system permanently confused, which researchers compare to living in a constant state of jet lag.","questions":[{"question_text":"What does the glymphatic system do during deep sleep?","options":["Replays daily experiences","Clears waste products from the brain","Produces sleep hormones","Strengthens muscles"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Why do students who sleep after studying perform better?","options":["They feel less anxious","Memories are transferred to long-term storage during sleep","They wake up earlier to revise","Sleep improves eyesight"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What does the lecturer say about people who sleep five hours?","options":["They perform worse but do not notice it","They perform as well as everyone else","They have more memory disorders","They need less regular schedules"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"According to the lecture, what matters most for healthy sleep?","options":["Sleeping longer at weekends","Avoiding all caffeine","Consistent bed and waking times","Sleeping in complete darkness"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"The lecturer compares an irregular sleep schedule to","options":["a cleaning crew","permanent jet lag","an overloaded library","a confused student"],"answers":["B"]}],"explanation":"Glymphatic system clears waste; hippocampus-to-cortex transfer explains study benefit; five-hour sleepers underperform unknowingly; regularity matters most; irregular schedule = constant jet lag."}',
4,'listening_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── LISTENING 4: Museum volunteer interview (Section 3 style) ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','listening','grouped_listening','Museum Volunteer Interview',
'{"type":"grouped_listening","group_name":"IELTS Listening Section 3 - Volunteer Interview","direction":"Listen to the conversation and answer the questions.","passage_script":"Man: Thanks for coming in, Sarah. You applied for the weekend volunteer role at the museum. Woman: Yes, I am studying history at the university, so it felt like a natural fit. Man: The role involves three things: greeting visitors, running the cloakroom, and helping with the family workshops on Sunday mornings. Woman: The workshops sound interesting. What do they involve? Man: Mostly craft activities linked to the current exhibition. Right now it is ancient Egypt, so children make paper models of pyramids. You would prepare materials and help the younger ones. Woman: I did something similar at a summer camp last year, so that suits me. Man: Good. Now, we ask volunteers for at least four hours each weekend, and we provide lunch vouchers and free entry to all paid exhibitions. Woman: Is there any training? Man: Yes, a full induction day on the fifteenth of March, covering fire safety, first aid basics, and how we handle school groups. Attendance is compulsory, I am afraid. Woman: That works. One question: is there any chance of paid work later? Man: Honestly, many of our paid guides started as volunteers, but I cannot promise anything.","questions":[{"question_text":"What subject is Sarah studying?","options":["Art","Education","History","Archaeology"],"answers":["C"]},{"question_text":"What do children currently make in the family workshops?","options":["Clay pots","Paper models of pyramids","Egyptian masks","Museum maps"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What is the minimum weekly time commitment?","options":["Three hours","Four hours each weekend","One full day","Sunday mornings only"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What happens on the fifteenth of March?","options":["The exhibition opens","A compulsory induction day","The first family workshop","A paid guide interview"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What does the interviewer say about paid work?","options":["A paid contract is guaranteed after one year","Volunteers can never become paid staff","Many paid guides began as volunteers, but nothing is promised","Paid roles are only for graduates"],"answers":["C"]}],"explanation":"History student; pyramid paper models; four hours per weekend; induction on 15 March compulsory; paid guides often ex-volunteers, no promise."}',
3,'listening_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');

-- ─── LISTENING 5: Lecture on plastic recycling (Section 4 style) ───
INSERT INTO test_contents (test_type, section, question_type, title, content, difficulty, topic, source, status) VALUES
('IELTS','listening','grouped_listening','Lecture: The Truth About Plastic Recycling',
'{"type":"grouped_listening","group_name":"IELTS Listening Section 4 - Plastic Recycling","direction":"Listen to the lecture and answer the questions.","passage_script":"Professor: The recycling symbol on plastic packaging is one of the most misunderstood images in modern life. Most consumers assume it means an item will be recycled. In reality, the number inside the triangle merely identifies the type of plastic, and of the seven categories, only two are widely recycled in practice: type one, used in drink bottles, and type two, found in milk jugs and detergent containers. The rest are technically recyclable but rarely economical to process. Why? Recycling plastic is essentially a quality problem. Unlike glass or aluminium, which can be melted and reformed indefinitely, plastic degrades each time it is processed. A bottle does not usually become another bottle; it becomes carpet fibre or a fleece jacket, and after that, nothing. Researchers call this downcycling. The economics make things worse. Sorting mixed plastic is labour intensive, and whenever the oil price falls, new plastic becomes cheaper than recycled material, and demand for the recycled product collapses. So what actually works? The evidence is clearest for deposit return schemes, where customers pay a small refundable charge on each bottle. Countries using them recover over ninety percent of drink containers, roughly double the rate of kerbside collection alone. The wider lesson, I would argue, is that recycling is the last resort in the waste hierarchy, not the first: reducing and reusing packaging prevents waste that recycling can only manage.","questions":[{"question_text":"What does the number inside the recycling triangle actually indicate?","options":["How many times the item can be recycled","The type of plastic","The recycling centre code","The percentage of recycled content"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"Why is plastic recycling described as a quality problem?","options":["Plastic degrades each time it is processed","Plastic is too light to sort","Consumers wash bottles poorly","Factories refuse mixed colours"],"answers":["A"]},{"question_text":"What happens to demand for recycled plastic when oil prices fall?","options":["It rises sharply","It collapses","It stays the same","It shifts to glass"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What recovery rate do deposit return schemes achieve for drink containers?","options":["About half","Over ninety percent","Around seventy percent","Almost forty percent"],"answers":["B"]},{"question_text":"What is the lecturer''s main conclusion?","options":["Recycling should be the first priority","All plastic types should be banned","Reducing and reusing matter more than recycling","Kerbside collection should be expanded"],"answers":["C"]}],"explanation":"Number identifies plastic type; degradation = downcycling quality problem; cheap oil kills recycled demand; deposit schemes recover 90%+; reduce and reuse before recycle."}',
4,'listening_strategy','seed_handwritten','published');
